  "summary": "A regional variation of Gothic architecture using brick instead of stone in areas lacking natural stone resources.",
  "description": "It adapts traditional Gothic forms like pointed arches and ribbed vaults to brick construction. This style developed in regions with limited access to quality stone, creating a distinctive aesthetic with red brick facades.",
  "culturalContext": "Brick Gothic (Backsteingotik) — the North German and Baltic adaptation of Gothic where stone was absent — built Lübeck's Holstentor, Gdańsk's St. Mary's (largest brick church on earth), Stralsund and Rostock townscapes from the Hanseatic trade's clay: glazed brick color fields, stepped gables, tiered towers, and tracery translated into molded brick. The style defined the \"Hanseatic\" look across Germany, Poland and the Baltics; UNESCO cities (Lübeck, Stralsund-Wismar, Toruń) preserve its austere skyline.",
